mc_snooping: pull the MLD group address before reading it

mc_ipv6_rcv() validates eight bytes:

	if (unlikely(!pskb_may_pull(skb2, offset + sizeof(*icmp6h))))
		goto out;

then casts that header to struct mld_msg - icmp6hdr plus a 16-byte
in6_addr - and hands &mld->mld_mca to mc_ipv6_report() (ICMPV6_MGM_REPORT)
and mc_ipv6_leave_group() (ICMPV6_MGM_REDUCTION). Both read the group
address at offset 8..23: a multicast test, MC_CHECK_TBIT(), and a full
16-byte copy into the mdb key. Nothing checks those bytes are present, so
an ICMPv6 type 131 or 132 message that is exactly eight bytes long reads
16 bytes past the end of the frame, and the result becomes the group hash
key an unauthenticated client just installed.

Found by a coverage-guided ASan harness over mc_rcv(); a 70-byte frame
reproduces it - IPv6 payload_len 16, an 8-byte hop-by-hop router alert,
then `83 00 00 00 00 00 00 00`. On hardware the read lands in skb
tailroom rather than faulting, which makes it a disclosure of whatever
the buffer last held into the snooping table rather than a panic; when
the frame carries trailing bytes, the pskb_trim_rcsum() above trims
skb->len to the attacker's declared payload_len, so the bytes read past
the logical packet are attacker-supplied.

The two siblings are already correct, which is what makes this a gap
rather than a design: mc_ipv4_rcv() pulls the whole struct igmphdr
including ->group, and mc_ipv6_query() guards with
skb->len == sizeof(*mld).

The pull cannot simply be widened - an MLDv2 report with no group records
is legitimately eight bytes and shares this path - so make it conditional
on the two types that read the address, and re-derive the header
afterwards because pskb_may_pull() may reallocate.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>

--- a/mc_snooping.c
+++ b/mc_snooping.c
@@ -2536,6 +2536,20 @@
 	MC_SKB_CB(skb)->igmp = 1;
 	MC_SKB_CB(skb)->mdb = NULL;
 
+	/*
+	 * Only struct icmp6hdr was pulled above. The report and done arms below
+	 * read struct mld_msg, whose 16-byte group address sits past it, while
+	 * an MLDv2 report is legitimately just those 8 bytes - so the pull has
+	 * to be conditional on the type rather than simply larger.
+	 */
+	if ((icmp6h->icmp6_type == ICMPV6_MGM_REPORT ||
+			icmp6h->icmp6_type == ICMPV6_MGM_REDUCTION) &&
+			!pskb_may_pull(skb2, sizeof(struct mld_msg))) {
+		err = -EINVAL;
+		goto out;
+	}
+
+	icmp6h = icmp6_hdr(skb2);
 	mld = (struct mld_msg *)icmp6h;
 	err = 0;
 	switch (icmp6h->icmp6_type) {
